    (v_topic_id, 'A 62-old gentleman is evaluated in the emergency department after being punched in the left orbit during an altercation. The patient reports dull orbital pain and binocular double vision, particularly when looking upwards. Physical examination reveals periorbital ecchymosis and edema. Ophthalmic examination findings include:<br>• Extraocular movements: <b>Severe restriction of upward gaze in the affected eye, with reproduction of vertical diplopia</b><br>• Sensory testing: <b>Marked numbness/decreased sensation to light touch over the ipsilateral cheek, upper lip, and lateral nose</b><br>• Palpation: <b>Subcutaneous crepitus over the infraorbital rim upon blowing his nose</b><br>• Intraocular pressure: <b>16 mmHg</b><br><br>What is the most likely diagnosis, which structure is classically entrapped, and what is the most appropriate initial management?', '["Retrobulbar hemorrhage; entrapment of the lateral rectus muscle; initiate high-dose IV methylprednisolone and immediate lateral canthotomy", "Orbital blowout fracture (orbital roof); entrapment of the superior rectus muscle and herniation into the frontal sinus; perform emergent surgical decompression within 6 hours", "Traumatic orbital cellulitis; entrapment of the inferior oblique muscle; initiate IV broad-spectrum vancomycin and ceftriaxone, and apply a pressure patch", "Orbital blowout fracture (orbital floor); entrapment of the inferior rectus muscle and herniation of orbital fat into the maxillary sinus; prescribe oral antibiotics, nasal decongestants, and strictly instruct the patient to avoid blowing {possessive} nose", "Zygomaticomaxillary complex fracture; entrapment of the medial rectus muscle; instruct the patient to perform daily eye exercises and blow {possessive} nose aggressively"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This patient presents with a classic **orbital blowout fracture**, most commonly involving the **orbital floor** (composed of the thin maxillary bone). Blunt force to the orbit increases intraorbital pressure, fracturing the thin floor and causing herniation of orbital contents. Key diagnostic features include: (1) **vertical diplopia on upward gaze** due to **entrapment of the inferior rectus muscle** and orbital fat within the fracture site, (2) **hypoesthesia of the ipsilateral cheek, upper lip, and lateral nose** due to injury of the **infraorbital nerve (CN V2)** which courses through the orbital floor, and (3) **subcutaneous emphysema/crepitus** that occurs when air from the maxillary sinus is forced into the orbit (classically triggered by **blowing the nose**). Initial management is conservative: (1) **strictly instruct the patient to avoid blowing their nose** to prevent forcing sinus bacteria and air into the orbit (which could cause orbital compartment syndrome), (2) **oral antibiotics** (e.g., Amoxicillin-Clavulanate) to cover sinus pathogens, (3) **nasal decongestants** (e.g., Oxymetazoline) to facilitate sinus drainage, and (4) outpatient ophthalmology/maxillofacial follow-up. Surgical repair is indicated if there is persistent diplopia, significant enophthalmos (>2 mm), or CT evidence of a large fracture.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 52-old male reports photophobia and a highly bothersome ''double vision'' in the right eye 50 days after being struck by a high-speed squash ball. Crucially, when the patient closes his uninjured eye, the double vision persists (monocular diplopia). Ophthalmic examination reveals:<br>• Best-corrected visual acuity: <b>20/40</b><br>• Slit-lamp biomicroscopy: <b>A D-shaped pupil in the affected eye, and a distinct, dark, crescent-shaped gap at the peripheral superior limbus, exposing the underlying lens equator</b><br>• Lens/Fundus: <b>The crystalline lens is clear and stable; dilated fundus examination is normal; no retinal tears are present</b><br>• Intraocular pressure: <b>16 mmHg</b><br><br>What is the most likely diagnosis, what is the pathophysiology of the patient''s double vision, and what is the treatment of choice?', '["Open globe injury; rupture of the scleral wall near the limbus; place a tight pressure patch, administer IV cefazolin, and perform emergent vitrectomy", "Posterior synechiae; adhesion of the iris to the anterior lens capsule causing monocular diplopia; treat with aggressive topical pilocarpine and surgical pupilloplasty", "Traumatic iridodialysis; tear of the iris sphincter muscle causing a second pupil; manage with emergent laser peripheral iridotomy within 24 hours", "Traumatic iridodialysis; separation of the iris root from the ciliary body creating an accessory optical axis (second pupil) causing monocular diplopia; manage conservatively with cycloplegics/tinted contact lenses, or perform surgical iridopexy if highly symptomatic", "Subluxated crystalline lens (ectopia lentis); disruption of the ciliary zonules causing asymmetric refraction; perform emergent lens extraction and PCIOL exchange"]'::jsonb, 3, 'This patient has a **traumatic iridodialysis**, which is the localized separation of the **iris root from the ciliary body**, typically caused by severe blunt trauma. The separation creates a peripheral gap or ''second pupil'' (accessory optical axis). When light enters the eye through both the normal pupil and the peripheral iridodialysis gap, two separate images are projected onto the retina, resulting in **monocular diplopia** (double vision that persists when the opposite eye is closed). Slit-lamp findings classically reveal a **D-shaped pupil** and a visible peripheral crescent gap. Management depends on symptoms: small, asymptomatic iridodialyses require only observation. Mild cases with photophobia or mild glare can be managed conservatively with tinted/cosmetic contact lenses or sunglasses. Large, highly symptomatic iridodialyses causing disabling glare, monocular diplopia, or cosmetic defects are treated with **surgical iridopexy** (suturing the iris root back to the ciliary body scleral spur).'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 71-old female is brought to the emergency department with progressive, excruciating pain and rapidly worsening vision in the right eye 1 hour after sustaining blunt facial trauma in a motor vehicle collision. Ophthalmic examination reveals:<br>• Eyelids: <b>Marked ecchymosis, extreme swelling, and a rock-hard orbit ( eyelids are tense, rigid, and resistant to manual opening or retropulsion)</b><br>• Pupil: <b>A 7-mm fixed, dilated pupil with a prominent relative afferent pupillary defect (RAPD)</b><br>• Visual acuity: <b>Light perception (LP) only</b><br>• Intraocular pressure: <b>54 mmHg</b> (normal 10-21 mmHg)<br><br>What is the most likely diagnosis, and what is the immediate, gold-standard, life-and-sight-saving intervention?', '["Open globe injury; immediate placement of a rigid eye shield, administration of IV antibiotics, and emergent anterior chamber washout", "Retrobulbar hemorrhage / Orbital Compartment Syndrome (OCS); immediate bedside lateral canthotomy and cantholysis (LCC) without waiting for imaging", "Traumatic carotid-cavernous fistula; immediate endovascular embolization of the internal carotid artery", "Retrobulbar hemorrhage; emergent maxillofacial CT scan to plan surgical bony decompression, combined with high-dose IV methylprednisolone", "Acute angle-closure glaucoma; immediate laser peripheral iridotomy and administration of IV mannitol and topical timolol"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This patient presents with a **retrobulbar hemorrhage causing Orbital Compartment Syndrome (OCS)**. The orbit is a rigid, bony cavity bounded anteriorly by the orbital septum. Hemorrhage or edema within this closed space rapidly increases intraorbital pressure. When the pressure exceeds central retinal artery perfusion pressure, ischemia of the optic nerve and retina ensues, leading to permanent blindness within 90-120 minutes. Key diagnostic signs include a **rock-hard orbit on palpation** (tense, rigid eyelids resistant to manual opening), **progressive proptosis**, severe visual decline, **RAPD**, a fixed/dilated pupil, and a **severely elevated IOP (often >40-50 mmHg)**. **OCS is a clinical diagnosis and an absolute surgical emergency**. The immediate, gold-standard treatment is a bedside **lateral canthotomy and cantholysis (LCC)**, which releases the lateral palpebral ligament and allows the orbital contents to expand anteriorly, immediately lowering intraorbital pressure. **Imaging (such as a CT scan) or medical therapy must NEVER delay LCC**.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 48-old female presents with progressive blurring of vision, floaters, and photophobia in her left eye over the past 52 weeks. Ophthalmic history is notable for a penetrating, open-globe injury to the **contralateral** eye 5 months ago, which resulted in complete permanent blindness in that eye. Ophthalmic examination of the **previously uninjured, contralateral** eye reveals:<br>• Visual acuity: <b>20/100</b><br>• Slit-lamp biomicroscopy: <b>Moderate conjunctival injection, ciliary flush, keratic precipitates on the corneal endothelium, severe anterior chamber cell and flare, and posterior synechiae</b><br>• Dilated fundus examination: <b>Bilateral vitritis, optic disc edema, and multiple small, yellowish-white nodules scattered throughout the peripheral retina (Dalen-Fuchs nodules)</b><br><br>What is the most likely diagnosis, what is the underlying pathophysiology, and how could this condition have been prevented?', '["Vogt-Koyanagi-Harada (VKH) syndrome; systemic autoimmune melanocortin attack; prevented by aggressive prophylactic systemic cyclosporine and topical prednisone", "Sympathetic ophthalmia; bacterial endophthalmitis crossing the optic chiasm via retrochiasmatic pathways; prevented by high-dose broad-spectrum IV ceftriaxone at the time of injury", "Traumatic sympathetic uveitis; delayed hypersensitivity reaction to intraocular retained wood foreign body; prevented by immediate MRI imaging and surgical extraction", "Phacoanaphylactic endophthalmitis; autoimmune reaction to lens proteins exposed during trauma; prevented by immediate posterior chamber intraocular lens (PCIOL) implantation", "Sympathetic ophthalmia; bilateral granulomatous uveitis caused by a T-cell mediated autoimmune response to sequestered retinal antigens released during trauma; prevented by enucleation of the injured eye within 2 weeks of the initial trauma"]'::jsonb, 4, '**Sympathetic Ophthalmia** is a rare, bilateral, granulomatous uveitis that occurs weeks to years (most commonly within 3 months) following a penetrating open-globe injury to **one** eye (the **''exciting'' eye**). The pathophysiology is **autoimmune**: the initial trauma disrupts the blood-ocular barrier, exposing **sequestered retinal self-antigens** (which are normally immunologically privileged) to the lymphatic system. This triggers a **T-cell mediated autoimmune attack** against the uveal tract in both the traumatized exciting eye and the previously uninjured **contralateral ''sympathizing'' eye**. Classic findings include keratic precipitates, vitritis, optic disc edema, and **Dalen-Fuchs nodules** (epithelioid cell clusters between the RPE and Bruch''s membrane). Sympathetic ophthalmia can be **prevented by early enucleation of the severely traumatized, blind exciting eye within 2 weeks of the initial injury** (before autoimmune sensitization occurs). Once sympathetic ophthalmia has developed in the sympathizing eye, enucleation of the exciting eye is no longer helpful, and the patient must be treated with aggressive, long-term **systemic corticosteroids and immunosuppressants**.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 36-old gentleman is evaluated in the emergency department following high-impact blunt head trauma after a fall from a height of 13 feet, striking his ipsilateral forehead and brow. The patient reports sudden, profound loss of vision in the right eye. Ophthalmic examination findings include:<br>• Best-corrected visual acuity: <b>20/400</b> in the affected eye (20/20 in contralateral eye)<br>• Pupillary examination: <b>A positive relative afferent pupillary defect (RAPD) in the affected eye</b><br>• Color vision: <b>Severe dyschromatopsia (red-green color desaturation; red caps appear grey)</b><br>• Dilated funduscopy: <b>The optic disc, macula, and peripheral retina appear completely normal and healthy</b><br>• Intraocular pressure: <b>22 mmHg</b><br><br>What is the most likely diagnosis, and what is the underlying mechanism?', '["Traumatic optic neuropathy (indirect TON); shearing of the intracanalicular optic nerve fibers or microvascular blood supply due to force transmitted through the orbital bones; initial optic disc is normal, with pallor developing over 4-6 weeks", "Central retinal artery occlusion (CRAO); embolic obstruction of the central retinal artery at the optic disc; fundus examination classically reveals a pale retina with a cherry-red spot at the fovea", "Retinal detachment; rhegmatogenous tear of the peripheral sensory retina; fundus examination classically reveals a elevated, grey, undulating retina with a horseshoe tear", "Traumatic optic neuropathy (direct TON); direct laceration of the intraocular optic disc by a bony fragment; fundus examination classically reveals immediate massive disc edema and vitreous hemorrhage", "Retrobulbar hemorrhage; orbital compartment syndrome compressing the orbital veins; fundus examination reveals severe optic nerve swelling and diffuse flame hemorrhages"]'::jsonb, 0, 'This patient presents with **indirect Traumatic Optic Neuropathy (TON)**. Indirect TON is caused by blunt head trauma (classically a force transmitted from an impact to the forehead, brow, or temple) where the shockwave is transmitted through the orbital bones to the **optic canal**, resulting in **shearing of the intracanalicular optic nerve fibers** or disruption of its delicate microvascular blood supply. Clinical hallmarks include: (1) **sudden, severe vision loss** immediately following blunt trauma, (2) **severe dyschromatopsia** (color desaturation, classically checked by asking the patient to view a red cap), and (3) a **positive relative afferent pupillary defect (RAPD)**. Crucially, **dilated funduscopy is completely normal initially**, because the injury occurs posteriorly within the optic canal (retrobulbar region). Over **4 to 6 weeks**, retrograde degeneration occurs, and the patient develops **optic disc pallor/atrophy**. Diagnostic evaluation includes thin-cut CT scanning of the orbits to evaluate the optic canal for fractures or bony fragments.'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 60-old woman presents with a 55-day history of dull, aching pain, severe photophobia, tearing, and mild blurry vision in the right eye. The symptoms began 4 days after she was struck in the eye by a fist. Ophthalmic examination reveals:<br>• Best-corrected visual acuity: <b>20/25</b><br>• Conjunctiva: <b>Mild circumcorneal conjunctival injection (ciliary flush)</b><br>• Slit-lamp biomicroscopy: <b>Cornea is clear; 2+ cells and 1+ flare are visible in the anterior chamber; the pupil is mildly constricted (miotic) and sluggishly reactive to light; no hypopyon or fibrin is present</b><br>• Intraocular pressure: <b>15 mmHg</b> (normal 10-21 mmHg)<br>• Dilated fundus examination: <b>Normal posterior segment, clear vitreous, no macular edema</b><br><br>What is the most likely diagnosis, and what is the standard first-line management?', '["Primary open-angle glaucoma; treat with topical latanoprost drops and systemic acetazolamide, and avoid pupillary dilation", "Traumatic iridocyclitis (traumatic anterior uveitis); treat with topical cycloplegics (e.g., cyclopentolate) to relieve ciliary spasm pain and topical corticosteroids (e.g., prednisolone acetate) to suppress sterile inflammation", "Corneal abrasion; treat with topical ciprofloxacin ointment, a tight pressure patch, and avoid topical steroids", "Acute postoperative endophthalmitis; treat with emergent vitreous tap and intravitreal broad-spectrum vancomycin and ceftazidime injections", "Toxic Anterior Segment Syndrome (TASS); treat with immediate hourly topical prednisolone acetate and systemic broad-spectrum levofloxacin"]'::jsonb, 1, 'This patient presents with **traumatic iridocyclitis (also known as traumatic anterior uveitis)**. This is a sterile inflammatory response of the iris and ciliary body that typically develops **1 to 3 days following blunt trauma**. Clinical findings include a dull, aching ocular pain, severe photophobia, **ciliary flush** (circumcorneal conjunctival injection), **anterior chamber cell and flare** (due to protein and inflammatory cells leaking from injured iris blood vessels), and a **sluggish, mildly constricted (miotic) pupil** caused by iris sphincter spasm. Standard first-line management is twofold: (1) **topical cycloplegics** (e.g., Cyclopentolate, Homatropine) to dilate the pupil, which prevents the formation of posterior synechiae and immobilizes the iris/ciliary muscle to **relieve the painful ciliary spasm**, and (2) **topical corticosteroids** (e.g., Prednisolone acetate 1%) to suppress the sterile anterior segment inflammation. Symptoms typically resolve within a few weeks with gradual tapering of the corticosteroid drops.');
